WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.650
New information changes a probability.

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:05.070
When you are told something about the result,

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.710
cross out every outcome that no longer fits.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.290
What is left is your new, smaller world,

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:11.930
and you count inside it.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.800
The usual mistake is to keep the old total on the bottom

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:16.370
of the fraction.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:19.840
After information arrives, the total is the number of

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:20.910
outcomes that survive.

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:23.320
A fair die is rolled.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.440
You are told the result is even.

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:28.200
What is the probability that it is greater than three?

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:30.900
Pause and cross out what cannot have happened.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:36.430
The die shows one to six.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:38.930
"Even" removes one, three and five.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.990
Three outcomes survive: two, four and six.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:43.870
They are still equally likely.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.670
Of those three, the numbers greater than three are

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:48.600
four and six.

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:51.500
So the probability is two out of three.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.690
Without the information it would have been three out of

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.100
six, one half.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.360
The information changed the answer.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:00.980
Roll two fair dice separately.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.140
You are told the total is eight.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:07.020
Five pairs survive: two and six, three and five,

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:10.260
four and four, five and three, six and two.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.580
What is the chance that both dice show the same number?

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:17.060
Only four and four works: one out of five.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.300
Crossing out works because outcomes that were equally

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:22.580
likely before the information are still

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:23.970
equally likely afterwards.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:26.950
If the outcomes are not equally likely,

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.400
you compare their probabilities rather than

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:30.350
just counting them.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.330
Cross out what the information rules out.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.030
Then count favourable outcomes among the survivors.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:40.060
Notebook challenge: a die is rolled and you are told the

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:41.470
number is greater than two.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:43.730
What is the probability that it is odd?
